course: dictionary
lesson: 4414
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The right meaning depends on the context, which means the words and sentences around the word. «Halo» is one of those words.
explain: In a picture of an angel, a halo is a ring of light round the head. «A pale halo shone around the Moon last night» means a ring of light round the Moon, made by tiny ice crystals high in the sky. Look at what the sentence is about to choose the right meaning.
explain: Grown-ups also use halo in a joking way. «His halo slipped» means someone who seems perfectly good has done something naughty. «The halo effect» is when liking someone makes us believe everything they say. Ask yourself: is this sentence about light, a picture, or behaviour?
fact: A halo is a ring or circle of light.
fact: Halos are often drawn around the heads of holy people and angels in pictures.
fact: A halo around the Sun or Moon is made by ice crystals in the sky.
fact: «Halo» can be informal: «her halo slipped» means she did something naughty.
fact: The plural of halo can be haloes or halos.
exercise: In the painting, the angel had a bright halo above her head. What is a halo here? | A large grey cloud | *A ring of light round the head | A ring of gold worn on a finger | A kind of hat
exercise: «The halo effect makes us believe everything a liked person says.» What does the halo effect describe? | A sore place on the head | *Liking someone so much that we believe what they say | A ring of light round the Moon | A naughty child who hides a secret
exercise: «His halo slipped when he took the last biscuit.» What does his halo slipping mean? | He prayed very loudly | His hat fell off his head | He lost a ring of light | *He did something naughty
test: «A pale halo shone around the Moon on the cold night.» Where did the halo appear? | On a painting of a saint | On the ground under a street lamp | *In the sky, as a ring of light round the Moon | Around a child's head at school
test: Ellie says «her halo slipped» means her head fell off. Is Ellie right? | No, it means she has a new hat | Yes, a halo is part of the head | *No, it means someone who seemed good did something naughty | Yes, it means a head fell off
exam: «Her halo slipped» and «a halo around the Moon» both use halo. Why is the meaning different? | *The context, the words around it, shows which meaning fits | The Moon is a holy person | Halo has only one meaning, so one sentence must be wrong | The words are spelled differently
